### 6.2 Create Shipment

Guided, grouped form with four sections.

- **A. Shipment details:** name (required), auto-generated ID (`CC-###`, read-only), product category (Vaccine, Medical Supplies, Temperature-Sensitive Food, Pharmaceutical Product, Other), optional description.
- **B. Journey:** origin and destination (required; dropdown of the demo cities, see section 8; free text allowed but must warn "No route data for this pair" and block analysis), departure date and time, maximum acceptable transit time (hours).
- **C. Temperature requirements:** min, max, unit (Celsius), limits source or reference, optional notes. Offer a **"Demo preset 2 to 8 C"** chip that fills the values and labels them "Demo assumption". Helper text: limits must come from the product's own specification. Validate min < max and numeric range. Never silently assume a category implies a range.
- **D. Container and cooling (model inputs for a future simulation, not measurements):** insulation (Basic, Moderate, High), cooling (Active refrigerated, Passive), optional notes. Use sensible defaults so no engineering input is required.

Buttons: Cancel, Save Draft, **Generate Route Analysis** (validates, saves, then navigates to `/shipments/:id/analysis`). Inline errors, focus on the first invalid field, no `alert()`. Values persist when navigating away and back (see section 9).

### 6.3 Route Comparison and Simulation (hero screen, `/shipments/:id/analysis`)

**Layout (desktop):** header strip on top (shipment name, origin to destination, analysis status, Edit Shipment, **Run Demo Analysis**). Below, a two-column grid: **map on the left (about 60%)**, **two route cards stacked on the right**. Below the grid, full width: **Simulated Temperature Over Time** chart for the selected route with a small summary row. On tablet, stack map above cards. On mobile, a single column with the cards before the map.

- **Map:** Leaflet with OSM tiles, origin and destination markers, two polylines in Route A and Route B colors, selected route drawn thicker, legend, zoom controls. Polylines come from bundled precomputed geometry (section 8) and are captioned "Illustrative route geometry". Clicking a polyline or marker selects the route and shows a small popup. If tiles fail, show a tile-less panel with the same polylines on a plain background and the note "Map tiles unavailable".
- **Route cards:** name, distance, estimated travel time, simulation status. Before analysis: "Awaiting simulation." After: thermal-risk label (Low, Medium, High, each with the mercury micro-icon and text), excursion status, and View Details. Selecting a card highlights it and the map route, and they stay synchronized.
- **Chart:** time in hours on x, degrees C on y, the simulated trend line, upper and lower limit lines, a shaded permitted band, tooltips with elapsed time and temperature. Series named "Simulated estimate". Before analysis, show an informative placeholder. Excursion segments use the hatched band and a legend entry.
- **Summary row:** max simulated temperature, min simulated temperature, excursion count, total minutes outside range, constraint status. Each shows "Not yet evaluated" until analysis runs.
- States: loading (skeleton shimmer while the engine runs), empty, and error (AttentiveFace plus Retry).
- After a run, a link or button goes to **View Detailed Results**.

### 6.4 Detailed Results and Recommendation (`/shipments/:id/results`)

Two route result cards side by side (distance, travel time, max and min simulated temperature, excursions, constraint status, View Temperature Graph) and a **Recommendation panel**.

The Recommendation panel shows: preferred route (or "No feasible route found under the current assumptions."), reasons as a checklist, trade-offs (for example "20 minutes longer"), detected simulated excursions, and a **Limitations** list (demo model, unvalidated inputs, no live data). Wording example: "Route B is preferred in this demo because it meets the configured limits and has a lower simulated excursion risk under the selected assumptions." Generate this text only from engine output. Buttons: Test What-If Scenarios, Save to History (sets status), Back to Analysis.

### 6.5 What-If Scenario Simulator (`/shipments/:id/scenarios`)

Two columns on desktop, stacked on mobile.

- **Left, controls:** route select, additional delay slider (0 to 180 min, step 5), ambient temperature slider (15 to 45 C), cooling interruption toggle, interruption duration (0 to 120 min, shown only when the toggle is on), optional insulation preset. Current value displayed beside every control, with a one-line plain-English hint. Controls update state instantly. Buttons: **Run Scenario**, **Reset**, **Compare Against Baseline**.
- **Right, results:** Baseline vs Modified cards (journey time, max temperature, outside-range status), a comparison chart with both curves on one fixed y-axis and labeled "Baseline" and "Modified", and a one-sentence explanation of what changed. Use hedged language ("under these model assumptions"). Never promise that a delay will cause an excursion.
- Baseline here must equal the Route Analysis result for the same route and inputs.

### 6.6 Screen 6: History and Reports (`/history`)

**Layout (desktop):** header (title, short description, **Export CSV** button). Below, a two-column grid: **left (about 70%)** holds the filter bar and the results table; **right (about 30%)** holds an **Import card** containing the **Import Chest** (Appendix B) with a short instruction line underneath. On tablet and mobile the Import card collapses into a titled, collapsible section above the table, closed by default.

**Filters:** search box, status filter, product category filter, optional date filter. All work together. Table columns: ID (with copy icon), name, route, category, status, date, provenance tag (Demo, Simulated, or Imported). Rows open Shipment Details. On mobile, rows become cards. No results: AttentiveFace with "No matching shipments." and a Clear filters button.

**Export CSV** downloads a real file with the columns `id,name,category,route,status,date,provenance`. Guard against spreadsheet formula injection by prefixing any cell that begins with `=`, `+`, `-`, or `@` with a single quote. Do not show a PDF button.

**Import (the Import Chest):** accepts a `.csv` produced by the Export button above (same columns, header row required).

- Parse with your own small CSV parser (quoted fields, commas, newlines inside quotes). Limits: file at most 1 MB, at most 500 rows. Treat every cell as plain text and never render it as HTML.
- Required columns: `id,name,category,route,status,date`. A missing column rejects the file with a clear message.
- Row validation: non-empty id and name, a category from the allowed list (otherwise "Other"), a status from the allowed list (otherwise "Draft"), a parseable date. Skip duplicate IDs (including IDs already in the app) and invalid rows.
- Imported rows become shipment records with `isDemoRecord: false`, `source: "imported"`, provenance **"User-imported (unverified)"**, no analysis results, and an activity entry "Imported from CSV". Never create simulated values for them.
- Show a result summary in a toast ("Imported 5 records, skipped 2") and an expandable "Skipped rows" list with the reason for each (inline in the Import card, not a modal).
- Chest states are driven by React state: idle, drag-over, importing, success, error (see Appendix B). Drag-and-drop works as well as click.
- Imported records persist in localStorage like all others, and a **Remove imported records** link in the Import card clears only them (with a confirmation modal).

### 6.7 Shipment Details and Settings (compact)

- **Shipment Details:** identity (with copy icon), journey, product category, temperature limits (with source and a Demo assumption tag where applicable), container assumptions, selected route, status, latest activity (from the activity log), analysis summary if present, provenance tag. Actions depend on status: Continue Planning, View Route Analysis, View Results, Edit Shipment, Run Scenarios. Optional **Assign Driver** opens a small modal that says "Demo only: no driver system connected".
- **Settings:** brand label, temperature unit (Celsius only for now), a **Reduce motion** toggle that overrides the system setting (defaults to the system value), and a **Reset demo data** button with a confirmation modal.

## 7. Engine boundary (the most important architectural rule)

All analysis goes through `src/engine/`. UI components never compute temperatures. They call one function and render its output.

```js
// engine/index.js
analyzeRoute({ shipment, route, scenario }) => AnalysisResult   // may be async

// AnalysisResult
{
  routeId: "route-a",
  scenario: { extraDelayMin: 0, ambientC: 32, coolingInterruptionMin: 0 },
  travelTimeMin: 180,
  series: [{ tMin: 0, tempC: 4.0 }, ...],          // regular spacing, at least 24 points
  limits: { minC: 2, maxC: 8 },
  excursions: [{ startMin: 120, endMin: 135, peakC: 9.1 }],
  maxC: 9.1, minC: 3.8,
  minutesOutOfRange: 15,
  constraintStatus: "fails" | "meets" | "not_evaluated",
  riskLevel: "low" | "medium" | "high",
  provenance: "demo-simulated",                      // never "measured"
  modelVersion: "demo-0.1"
}

// engine/routeScorer.js
rankRoutes(results[], constraints) => {
  preferredRouteId: string | null,                   // null means no feasible route
  reasons: string[], tradeoffs: string[], limitations: string[]
}
```

Files: `thermalModel.js`, `excursionDetector.js`, `routeScorer.js`, `scenarioEngine.js`, `index.js`. A future real engine or API client replaces `engine/index.js` and nothing else.

**Model rule:** `thermalModel.js` is a small, deterministic, transparent function (a simple lumped heat-gain curve driven by ambient temperature, insulation preset, cooling mode, travel time, and interruptions). Document its formula in a comment and label it "demo-0.1, not a validated thermal model". No randomness. Calibrate its presets so the golden path in section 8 reproduces within 0.1 C and 1 minute. If calibration cannot hit the golden numbers, use fixture lookup for the golden path only, and keep the model for all other inputs. Say so in the README.

## 8. Seed data and golden path

Keep data in `src/data/` (`demoShipments.js`, `demoRoutes.js`, `routeGeometry.js`, `demoScenarios.js`). Do not inline data in JSX.

**Cities with bundled route geometry:** Bengaluru to Mysuru, Chennai, Hyderabad, Pune, and Delhi to Jaipur. Each pair has two candidate routes with a precomputed simplified polyline (30 to 80 points) stored as static data, labeled "Illustrative route geometry". Never draw a straight line and call it a road. Never call a live routing service.

**Shipment record:**

```js
{
  id, name, category, description,
  origin, destination,
  departureDateTime, maxTransitHours,
  temperatureLimits: { minC, maxC, unit: "C", source, isDemoAssumption },
  container: { insulation, coolingSystem, notes },
  status,            // "Draft" | "Analyzed" | "Recommended" | "No Feasible Route"
  selectedRouteId, assignedDriver,   // assignedDriver is demo-only
  analysis,          // { [routeId]: AnalysisResult, ranking }
  activity: [{ at, text, provenance }],
  isDemoRecord,      // true for seeds, false for user-created and imported
  source,            // "demo" | "user" | "imported"
  createdAt, updatedAt
}
```

**Status transitions:** save without analysis gives Draft. Run Demo Analysis gives Analyzed, then Recommended if the scorer picks a route, or No Feasible Route if it returns null. Editing temperature or container inputs on an analyzed shipment resets it to Draft and clears stale results, with a toast explaining why.

**Golden path (CC-001, Vaccine Delivery 001, Bengaluru to Mysuru, 2 to 8 C demo preset, Moderate insulation, Active cooling, ambient 32 C):**

| | Route A (faster) | Route B |
|---|---|---|
| Distance | 145 km | 165 km |
| Travel time | 3 h 0 m | 3 h 20 m |
| Max simulated temperature | 9.1 C | 6.2 C |
| Excursions | 1 (15 min) | None |
| Risk label | Medium | Low |
| Constraint status | Does not meet limit | Meets configured limits |

Result: Route B preferred, with the reason "no simulated excursions, lower thermal risk, 20 minutes longer".

**Scenario case on Route A:** baseline 3 h 0 m, max 7.2 C, no excursions. Modified (delay 30 min, ambient 35 C, cooling interruption on): 3 h 30 m, max 10.5 C, excursion about 25 min. Treat these as test fixtures for the engine.

**Seed shipments:** CC-001 (above, Draft, so the demo starts with a fresh analysis), CC-002 Medical Supply 002 Bengaluru to Chennai (Analyzed), CC-003 Pharmaceutical Delivery 003 Bengaluru to Hyderabad (Recommended), and one **seeded No Feasible Route** shipment (for example CC-004, Bengaluru to Pune, Basic insulation, Passive cooling, narrow limits) so that state is exercised. All carry `isDemoRecord: true` and show a "Demo" tag.

## 9. State and persistence

- React Context plus `useReducer` in `context/ShipmentContext.jsx`. No state library.
- Persist shipments, drafts, imported records, settings, and the in-progress form to `localStorage` under a versioned key (for example `coldchain:v1`). Wrap reads and writes in try/catch, tolerate corrupt data by falling back to seed data, and provide Reset demo data in Settings.
- IDs are stable strings. Never look anything up by array index.
- Toasts (not alerts) for: draft saved, shipment saved, analysis completed, scenario updated, CSV exported, CSV imported, ID copied, validation errors. Auto-dismiss, `aria-live` polite, at most two visible.

